# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# WHAT THIS FILE IS, AND WHY IT IS NOT THE TOOLKIT'S LEDGER
#
# ASIC/asic-toolkit/test/MUTATION_COVERAGE declares how many planted faults the
# TOOLKIT's own suite must reject — its Tcl flow procs, its static gates, its
# stage scenarios. That toolkit is a pinned submodule shared with the compute
# chiplet, and its numbers are about code that is identical on both.
#
# This file is the other half, and it is deliberately HERE rather than there:
# ci/signoff.yaml is, in its own words, "THE ONLY PART THAT DIFFERS BETWEEN
# CHIPLETS". A gate matrix for these stages is a fact about THIS design, so
# recording it in the shared submodule would both bump a pinned pointer and make
# one chiplet's coverage look like the other's.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# HOW TO READ IT
#
#   PROVEN     the stage has a `check:` that re-reads the evidence, plus a
#              must_pass fixture AND at least one must_fail fixture, and
#              `scripts/ci/signoff.py prove` runs all of them. The gate is
#              demonstrated to say yes to one shape of evidence and no to
#              another.
#   RC-ONLY    the stage has no `check:`. Its verdict is the exit status of the
#              tool it invokes, and NOTHING in this repository demonstrates that
#              a bad result would produce a nonzero one. `prove` skips these
#              stages entirely — they do not appear in its case count, so the
#              green "N problem(s): 0" line says nothing whatever about them.
#
# A `report`-gate stage that is RC-ONLY costs a missing observation. A `block`
# stage that is RC-ONLY can pass a bad chip, which is why those are listed first
# and separately.
#
# THE LIMIT OF THE WORD "PROVEN". `prove` confirms a check is SELF-CONSISTENT
# WITH ITS FIXTURE. It does not confirm the fixture resembles the tool. A green
# prove run over an unrepresentative fixture is theatre, and this project has
# already shipped one: a hand-written 322-byte "aborted HAL run" with zero
# rule-tally lines, where a real 26 MB abort emits 40 — on the strength of which
# a load-bearing guard was documented as redundant. Fixture provenance is
# tracked in ci/fixtures/README.md and is the thing to distrust first.
#
# NOTHING EDITS THIS FILE AUTOMATICALLY. A coverage floor that the flow raises
# to whatever today's run produced is the exact defect this repository keeps
# finding in its own gates — an RV-via count set to the day's number, a DRC
# budget set to the day's number. Moving a row is the thirty seconds in which
# somebody reads the new case.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------


# === BLOCKING STAGES WITH NO PROOF THEY CAN FAIL ===========================
# These decide whether the chip is signed off, and their entire verdict is a
# process exit status. Closing these is the highest-value work available.
#
#   lec-selftest   ASIC/genus-innovus lec-selftest. Ironic: it is itself a
#                  mutation harness for the LEC gate, and nothing checks that it
#                  ran its case list. A selftest that silently executes ZERO
#                  cases exits 0 — precisely the hole rom-selftest's `check:`
#                  was added to close, still open here. It needs the same
#                  treatment: assert the case count from the artefact.
#   lec-pnr        holds a Conformal seat for up to six hours, and its log is
#                  not re-read. A stale logs/lec_pnr.log from a previous run
#                  satisfies nothing and is contradicted by nothing.
blocking-unproven       2

# === REPORT-ONLY STAGES WITH NO PROOF THEY CAN FAIL ========================
#   cdc             gate: report by design (findings not yet triaged to zero).
#   lvs-preflight   gate: report by design (procurement blocker, expected red).
# Both are RC-ONLY. Neither can currently distinguish "the tool ran and found
# things" from "the tool did not run", which is the same failure both of their
# HAL/Calibre siblings have already been caught making.
report-unproven         2

# === PROVEN GATES ==========================================================
# One row per stage with a `check:` + `check_proof:`, giving the number of
# must_fail fixtures. Asserted against ci/signoff.yaml by
# scripts/ci/check_mutation_coverage.py, red in BOTH directions: fewer than
# declared means a proof was dropped; more means one was added unreviewed.
#
# fixtures are under ci/fixtures/<stage>/. Every must_fail case below has been
# checked to trip exactly ONE clause of its guard unless noted — a fixture that
# trips three guards at once pins none of them.
#
# stage              must_fail cases
chip-boundary           5
lint                    5
elab                    3
elab-strict             5
regress                 4
rom-selftest            3
rom-content             4
rom-gds                 4
lec                     3
# 3 -> 4 on 2026-08-17: fail-waiver-stale, added by a concurrent session and
# read before this number was moved. The other three are fail-design-results,
# fail-saturated-cap and fail-no-summary.
drc                     4

# Total across the proven gates. `signoff.py prove` reports its own case count
# (must_pass + must_fail); this is the must_fail half, which is the half that
# demonstrates a gate can FAIL.
must-fail-cases-total   40

# Stages carrying a `check:` AND a complete proof (a must_pass plus at least one
# must_fail, every fixture present on disk). Cross-checked against the manifest
# so that adding a gate without a proof, or a proof without a gate, is visible
# here and not only in a transient prove run.
stages-with-check      10


